# Adjudicated gitleaks findings. One line per finding, in gitleaks'
# own fingerprint format: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<startline>
#
# Nothing goes in here that is actually a secret. A finding is listed
# only when the string it matched is public BY DESIGN and knowing it
# grants no access — and the reason is written down, because a
# suppression whose justification is missing is indistinguishable from
# one that was wrong.
#
# ── 2026-09-01: the IndexNow key ──────────────────────────────────────
# `generic-api-key` matched `kKey` in tools/indexnow_submit.dart. It is
# a 32-character hex string, which is exactly the shape of an API token,
# so the match is a good one — the rule is doing its job.
#
# It is not a secret. IndexNow proves site ownership the same way Search
# Console's meta tag does: by requiring the value to be PUBLICLY
# READABLE at a well-known url. Ours is served, deliberately, at
#   https://yahwehword.com/7cad63f32387c0af0b842b4015fb2636.txt
# and the file that serves it is committed at
#   web/7cad63f32387c0af0b842b4015fb2636.txt
# Hiding the constant while publishing the file would be theatre.
#
# What knowing it allows: submitting urls *on yahwehword.com* to
# IndexNow. Nothing else — it is not reused, it authenticates no
# account, and it reads no data. The worst a third party could do is
# nominate our own paths for crawling; junk paths answer 404 (see the
# /read/* and /sermons/* rules in netlify.toml). If that ever mattered,
# rotating it is a two-file change: replace the key file in web/ and the
# constant, then re-run the submitter, whose pre-flight fetch fails loudly
# if the two disagree.
#
# Scoped to the one commit that introduced it. gitleaks-action scans the
# push's commit range, so this entry covers a re-scan of history; the
# inline `gitleaks:allow` on the constant itself covers a scan of the
# working tree. Both are needed because they are different scans.
